UNIT sortedIndex
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic

SIG sortedIndex(array, value) -> number
# `array`  : the (assumed-sorted) array to inspect. May be null/undefined.
# `value`  : the value to locate an insertion point for. Any JS value.
# returns  : an integer index in the range 0 .. array.length (inclusive),
#            the LEFTMOST index at which `value` could be inserted to keep
#            `array` sorted. (retHighest is NOT exposed by sortedIndex; it is
#            permanently falsy here — every "retHighest ? .. : .." below
#            therefore takes the ELSE branch.)

DEPENDS-ON none
# Self-contained module, zero runtime dependencies. Internally inlines four
# private helpers (baseSortedIndex, baseSortedIndexBy, isObjectLike, isSymbol,
# identity). The blind re-emitter reconstructs them inline; nothing is imported.

# ============================================================================
# CONSTANTS (exact, from source)
# ============================================================================
# MAX_ARRAY_LENGTH      = 4294967295            (0xFFFFFFFF)
# MAX_ARRAY_INDEX       = MAX_ARRAY_LENGTH - 1  = 4294967294
# HALF_MAX_ARRAY_LENGTH = MAX_ARRAY_LENGTH >>> 1 = 2147483647
# symbolTag             = "[object Symbol]"
# (For ALL in-scope inputs the array length is tiny, so HALF/MAX thresholds
#  never bite, but they are part of the exact definition — see NODE A.)

# ============================================================================
# READ-ONLY GUARANTEE
# ============================================================================
# sortedIndex performs ONLY reads of `array` (length + indexed element access)
# and NEVER mutates `array`, `value`, or any global. It is a pure function of
# its two arguments. (No seam: no clock/RNG/env/io. ORACLE-CLASS deterministic.)

# ============================================================================
# HELPER: isSymbol(v)            # used in BOTH paths
# ============================================================================
#   isObjectLike(v) := (Boolean(v) === true) AND (typeof v === "object")
#                      # i.e. v is truthy AND typeof v is "object"
#                      # note: null -> false (typeof null is "object" but !!null is false)
#   isSymbol(v) := (typeof v === "symbol")
#                  OR ( isObjectLike(v) AND
#                       Object.prototype.toString.call(v) === "[object Symbol]" )
#   # Symbol primitives -> true. Symbol-wrapper objects -> true.
#   # Everything else (numbers, strings, null, undefined, NaN, bigint,
#   #   plain objects, arrays) -> false.

# ============================================================================
# HELPER: identity(v) := v       # the iteratee used by sortedIndex's general path
# ============================================================================
# Because the iteratee is identity, `iteratee(value) === value` and
# `iteratee(array[mid]) === array[mid]`. The general-path pseudo-code below is
# already written with identity folded in (so `computed` is simply `array[mid]`).

# ============================================================================
# BEHAVIOR (ordered)
# ============================================================================

BEHAVIOR

  # --- entry --------------------------------------------------------------
  # sortedIndex(array, value) := baseSortedIndex(array, value)   # retHighest omitted => undefined (falsy)

  # baseSortedIndex(array, value):
  #   low  := 0
  #   high := array ? array.length : 0
  #     # NOTE the truthiness test on `array`: a null OR undefined array yields
  #     # high = 0 (the loop never runs) => returns 0. (Verified: f(null,5)=0,
  #     # f(undefined,5)=0.) An EMPTY array also yields high = 0 => returns 0.

  NODE A  # PATH SELECTION (the single most important branch)
    # Take the NUMERIC FAST PATH iff ALL THREE hold:
    #     (typeof value === "number")
    #     AND (value === value)           # i.e. value is NOT NaN
    #     AND (high <= HALF_MAX_ARRAY_LENGTH)   # 2147483647; always true in-scope
    # Otherwise take the GENERAL PATH (NODE C).
    #
    # Consequences a blind re-emitter MUST honor:
    #   * value is a finite/infinite Number (incl. -0, +0, Infinity, -Infinity,
    #     but NOT NaN) -> NUMERIC PATH.
    #   * value is NaN -> typeof is "number" but value !== value -> GENERAL PATH.
    #   * value is a string / bigint / null / undefined / symbol / boxed Number
    #     (typeof "object") / object / array -> GENERAL PATH.

  NODE B  # NUMERIC FAST PATH (value is a non-NaN number)
    #   while (low < high):
    #       mid      := (low + high) >>> 1          # unsigned-right-shift-by-1 floor of the midpoint
    #       computed := array[mid]                  # raw element (NO iteratee)
    #       advance  := (computed !== null)
    #                   AND (isSymbol(computed) === false)
    #                   AND (computed < value)       # ELSE-branch of retHighest (strict "<")
    #       if advance: low  := mid + 1
    #       else:       high := mid
    #   return high
    #
    #   The guard `computed !== null && !isSymbol(computed)` means: if the
    #   element at mid is null OR a symbol, `advance` is FALSE -> high := mid
    #   (we move left past it). Otherwise the ordering is JS abstract relational
    #   `computed < value`:
    #     - number < number       -> numeric comparison
    #     - string < number       -> both coerced to Number; e.g. "2" < 2 is 2<2=false
    #     - undefined < number    -> Number(undefined)=NaN -> comparison is false
    #     - NaN-element < number   -> false (NaN compares false)
    #   In every "advance is false" case we set high := mid (move toward the
    #   leftmost qualifying position) -> this yields the LEFTMOST insertion index
    #   of any run of values equal to (or unordered-relative-to) `value`.

  NODE C  # GENERAL PATH  (= baseSortedIndexBy(array, value, identity, retHighest=undefined))
    # Precompute value-shape flags ONCE (with identity, value is unchanged):
    #   valIsNaN       := (value !== value)        # true only for NaN value
    #   valIsNull      := (value === null)
    #   valIsSymbol    := isSymbol(value)
    #   valIsUndefined := (value === undefined)
    #
    #   low  := 0
    #   high := array ? array.length : 0
    #   while (low < high):
    #       mid          := Math.floor((low + high) / 2)   # floored midpoint
    #       computed     := array[mid]                      # raw element (identity iteratee)
    #       othIsDefined := (computed !== undefined)
    #       othIsNull    := (computed === null)
    #       othIsReflexive := (computed === computed)        # false ONLY when element is NaN
    #       othIsSymbol  := isSymbol(computed)
    #
    #       # setLow chosen by the FIRST matching value-shape branch.
    #       # retHighest is falsy, so each "retHighest || X" reduces to X and
    #       # each "retHighest ? A : B" reduces to B. The reduced forms:
    #       if valIsNaN:
    #           setLow := othIsReflexive
    #               # advance past any NON-NaN element; stop (move left) at a NaN element.
    #       else if valIsUndefined:
    #           setLow := othIsReflexive AND othIsDefined
    #               # advance past any defined non-NaN element; stop at undefined or NaN.
    #       else if valIsNull:
    #           setLow := othIsReflexive AND othIsDefined AND (othIsNull === false)
    #               # advance past defined, non-NaN, non-null elements; stop at null/undefined/NaN.
    #       else if valIsSymbol:
    #           setLow := othIsReflexive AND othIsDefined AND (othIsNull === false)
    #                     AND (othIsSymbol === false)
    #               # advance past defined non-NaN non-null non-symbol; stop at symbol/null/undefined/NaN.
    #       else if (othIsNull OR othIsSymbol):
    #           setLow := false
    #               # value is an ordinary comparable (number-NaN already excluded, or
    #               # string/bigint/object/array); a null or symbol element is treated
    #               # as "not less than" -> move left.
    #       else:
    #           setLow := (computed < value)        # ELSE-branch of retHighest: strict "<"
    #               # JS abstract relational comparison:
    #               #   string < string -> lexicographic (UTF-16 code unit) order
    #               #   number-element  < string-value etc. -> ToNumber coercion
    #               #   bigint < bigint, bigint < number per spec
    #               #   any comparison involving NaN-element already handled by
    #               #     othIsReflexive? NO — for an ORDINARY value with a NaN element,
    #               #     othIsNull/othIsSymbol are false so we reach here and compute
    #               #     (NaN < value) which is false -> setLow false -> move left.
    #       if setLow: low  := mid + 1
    #       else:      high := mid
    #   return Math.min(high, MAX_ARRAY_INDEX)      # MAX_ARRAY_INDEX = 4294967294
    #       # the Math.min clamp is unreachable for in-scope (small) arrays; high
    #       # is always <= array.length << MAX_ARRAY_INDEX. Include it for fidelity.

  # --- LEFTMOST tie-break (applies to BOTH paths) -------------------------
  # The comparator advances `low` ONLY on a STRICT-less / "should-go-before"
  # condition and moves `high := mid` on equal-or-after. Therefore for any run
  # of elements equal to `value`, the returned index is the index of the FIRST
  # (leftmost) such element. (sortedLastIndex would use retHighest=true and "<=",
  # but sortedIndex never does.)

  # --- RETURN RANGE -------------------------------------------------------
  # The result is always an integer in [0, array.length] (or 0 for a
  # null/undefined array). Concretely:
  #   * empty / null / undefined array            -> 0
  #   * value < every element / "before" all      -> 0
  #   * value > every element / "after" all        -> array.length
  #   * otherwise                                  -> leftmost qualifying index.

ORACLE value -> /tmp/factory-run/lodash.sortedindex/sir/oracle.json
# Pure deterministic function. The oracle records value equivalence:
# expected = sortedIndex(array, value) compared structurally (an integer).

# ============================================================================
# WORKED EXAMPLES (verified by executing the real package)
# ============================================================================
# sortedIndex([30,50], 40)            => 1     # numeric path
# sortedIndex([], 5)                  => 0     # empty
# sortedIndex(null, 5)                => 0     # null array
# sortedIndex(undefined, 5)           => 0     # undefined array
# sortedIndex([10,20,30], 5)          => 0     # before all
# sortedIndex([10,20,30], 100)        => 3     # after all
# sortedIndex([10,20,30], 20)         => 1     # present (leftmost)
# sortedIndex([10,20,20,20,30], 20)   => 1     # duplicates leftmost
# sortedIndex([5], 5)                 => 0     # single elem equal -> 0
# sortedIndex([5], 3)                 => 0     # single smaller
# sortedIndex([5], 9)                 => 1     # single larger
# sortedIndex([1,2,3], NaN)           => 3     # NaN value, general path, all reflexive
# sortedIndex([1,2,NaN], NaN)         => 2     # stops at the NaN element
# sortedIndex([NaN,NaN], NaN)         => 0     # first non-reflexive element seen
# sortedIndex([1,2,3], null)          => 3     # null value, all defined-non-null
# sortedIndex([null,null,1], null)    => 0     # stops at leading null
# sortedIndex([1,2,3], undefined)     => 3     # undefined value
# sortedIndex([1,2,undefined], undefined) => 2 # stops at the undefined element
# sortedIndex(["a","b","c"], "b")     => 1     # general path, string present
# sortedIndex(["a","c"], "b")         => 1     # string absent, between
# sortedIndex(["a","b","b","c"], "b") => 1     # string duplicates leftmost
# sortedIndex(["apple","banana","cherry"], "blueberry") => 2  # lexicographic
# sortedIndex([null,1,2], 1)          => 0     # numeric path; null guard -> move left then 1<1 false
# sortedIndex([1,2,null], 5)          => 2     # numeric path; null at end ignored
# sortedIndex([1,S,3], 2)             => 1     # numeric path; symbol guard at mid -> move left (S=Symbol)
# sortedIndex([-Infinity,0,Infinity], 0) => 1  # numeric path with infinities
# sortedIndex([1,2,3], Infinity)      => 3
# sortedIndex([1,2,3], -Infinity)     => 0
# sortedIndex([0,0,0], -0)            => 0     # -0 is a number, value===value true
# sortedIndex([1,2,3], new Number(2)) => 1     # boxed Number is typeof object -> general path
# sortedIndex(["1","2","3"], 2)       => 1     # numeric value, string elems: "2"<2 -> 2<2 false
# sortedIndex([1,2,3], "2")           => 1     # string value, number elems: general path, 1<"2"->1<2 true
# sortedIndex([1,2,3], S)             => 3     # symbol value, all non-symbol non-null defined -> advance
# sortedIndex(["a",null,"z"], "m")    => 1     # null element, ordinary value -> setLow false at null
# sortedIndex([1n,2n,3n], 2n)         => 1     # bigint array+value, general path
# sortedIndex([1,2,3], 2n)            => 1     # bigint value, number elems, general path, 1<2n true

# ============================================================================
# SCOPE
# ============================================================================
SCOPE
  IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
    - Path selection (NODE A): typeof-number AND non-NaN AND length<=HALF.
    - Numeric fast path bisect (NODE B): mid=(low+high)>>>1, guard
      (computed!==null && !isSymbol(computed)) AND strict (computed < value).
    - General path bisect (NODE C): mid=Math.floor((low+high)/2), the full
      valIsNaN/valIsUndefined/valIsNull/valIsSymbol/(othIsNull||othIsSymbol)/else
      comparator matrix with retHighest folded to falsy; final Math.min clamp.
    - isSymbol / isObjectLike / identity helpers as defined above.
    - Leftmost tie-break; return range 0..length; null/undefined/empty array -> 0.
    - Read-only: no mutation of array/value.

  CARRIED DATA: none. (No large literal tables; only the 4 small numeric
    constants and the "[object Symbol]" tag string, all transcribed inline above.)

  OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (NOT emitted by genInputs; behavior still
  derivable from the spec above but excluded because the input codec cannot
  faithfully round-trip them):
    - Symbol primitive ELEMENTS or a Symbol VALUE (Symbol is not JSON/codec
      round-trippable). Their COMPARATOR behavior is fully specified above
      (isSymbol -> guard/branch), and the worked examples include S cases run
      against the real package, but genInputs does not emit Symbols.
    - Function elements / function value, boxed primitives (new Number/String),
      class instances: not codec-faithful -> excluded from genInputs. (A boxed
      Number value would take the GENERAL path since typeof is "object"; this is
      documented but not generated.)
    - Arrays whose length exceeds HALF_MAX_ARRAY_LENGTH (2147483647): out of
      practical envelope; the threshold branch is documented for completeness.
    - Non-array `array` arguments other than null/undefined (e.g. a string or
      object): the source uses `array ? array.length : 0` then indexes — out of
      the intended contract; genInputs emits only real arrays plus null/undefined.

PROVENANCE lodash.sortedindex@4.1.0
  repo:    lodash/lodash  (https://github.com/lodash/lodash)
  license: MIT  (jQuery Foundation; based on Underscore.js 1.8.3)
  source:  index.js  (module.exports = sortedIndex)
           Build: `lodash modularize exports="npm" -o ./`
  export:  DEFAULT export -> the blind re-emitter must `export default sortedIndex`.
